plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2 has "fearless ∧ noble" and "fearless ∧ noble → appeared_blessed" and "hardly_believed → ¬pity"; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2.2 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2.3 have "fearless ∧ noble" and "fearless ∧ noble → appeared_blessed" and "appeared_blessed → hardly_believed" and "hardly_believed → ¬pity"; they differ when only "Socrates died fearlessly", "his words and bearing were noble and gracious" and "to Phaedo, Socrates appeared blessed" are true, which the first allows
plato-phaedo-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2.2 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "Socrates died fearlessly", "his words and bearing were noble and gracious" and "to Phaedo, Socrates appeared blessed" are true and "Phaedo could hardly believe he was present at the death of a friend" and "Phaedo pitied Socrates" are false. Is S2, "For I could hardly believe that I was present at the death of a friend, and therefore I did not pity him, Echecrates; he died so fearlessly, and his words and bearing were so noble and gracious, that to me he appeared blessed.", true then?
plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2 has "appeared_blessed → hardly_believed"; plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2.2 and plato-phaedo-1.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "to Phaedo, Socrates appeared blessed" is true, which the second allows
